course: chinese-street
lesson: 2
title: The four tones: same sound, different meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In English you can say «cat» high, low, happy or grumpy and it is still a cat. In Mandarin the tune of a syllable is part of the word, like a vowel: change the tune and you change the word.
explain: There are four tones and a light one: mā stays high like a long recorder note, má rises like a surprised «huh?», mǎ dips low like a doubtful «mmm», mà drops like a firm «No!» to a dog, and the light ma has no tune at all.
explain: That is why Ming falls about laughing when you ask for táng (sweets) instead of tāng (soup), or count shǐ (poo) instead of shí (ten).
explain: Mix-ups are part of learning by ear; every learner makes them, and the friends' giggles are friendly. Listening hard, then copying the tune, is how you get it right.
fact: mā («mah» sung high and level) is the first tone, as in māma (mum); keep it high and don't let it fall.
fact: má («mah?» rising like «huh?») is the second tone; má means hemp and also the tingly feeling of Sichuan pepper, and the rise belongs to the word, not to a question.
fact: mǎ («mah» dipping low, then a little up) is the third tone, and mǎ means horse; in fast talk it often just goes low and stays there.
fact: mà («MAH!» falling sharply) is the fourth tone and means to scold; Chinese speakers are not angry, Mandarin just has many falling words.
fact: The light ma (a short «muh» with no tune) is the neutral tone; at the end of a sentence it turns it into a yes/no question: nǐ hǎo ma? is «are you well?».
fact: māma mà mǎ ma? means «is Mum scolding the horse?», a famous tone game friends use to test you, for fun and not to mock.
fact: tāng (high) is soup and táng (rising) is sugar or sweets.
fact: shuìjiào (both falling) is to sleep and shuǐjiǎo (both dipping) is boiled dumplings, the classic learner mix-up.
fact: wèn (falling) is to ask and wěn (dipping) is to kiss, so qǐng wèn (may I ask) must fall.
fact: mǎi (dipping) is to buy and mài (falling) is to sell; only the tone tells these opposites apart.
fact: shí (rising) is ten, shǐ (dipping) is poo, and shì (falling) is «is» or «yes».
fact: bāozi (high) is a filled steamed bun and bàozi (falling) is a leopard.
exercise: Which tone is mā (as in māma, mum)? | High and level | Rising | Dipping | Falling sharply
exercise: Which tone rises like a surprised «huh?» | The second tone, as in má | The first tone, as in mā | The fourth tone, as in mà | The light tone, as in ma
exercise: What does mǎ, with the dipping tone, mean? | Horse | Mum | To scold | Hemp
exercise: What does mà, falling sharply, mean? | To scold | Horse | Mum | A question word
exercise: What does the light ma do at the end of a sentence? | Turns it into a yes/no question | Makes it more polite to grown-ups | Means «mum» | Makes it past tense
exercise: What does the tongue-twister māma mà mǎ ma? mean? | Is Mum scolding the horse? | Mum, Mum, horse, horse | The horse is scolding Mum | Mum rides the horse home
exercise: You want soup. Which word do you ask for? | tāng, said high and level | táng, rising | tàng, falling | tǎng, dipping low and back up
exercise: «wǒ yào shuǐjiǎo» with both syllables dipping means | I want dumplings | I want to sleep | I want water | I want to go home
exercise: Which pair is «to ask» and «to kiss»? | wèn falling and wěn dipping | wēn high and wén rising | wén rising and wèn falling | wěn dipping and wēn high
exercise: «wǒ mài» with a falling tone means | I'm selling | I'm buying | I'm eating | I'm hungry
exercise: Counting ten, which tone must you use? | Rising: shí | Dipping: shǐ | Falling: shì | High: shī
exercise: At breakfast you ask for a bàozi with a falling tone. What did you ask for? | A leopard | A filled steamed bun | A newspaper | A bowl of soup
exercise: Why does tone matter more in Mandarin than in English? | The tune of a syllable is part of the word | Mandarin speakers are always singing as they talk | Only questions use tones in Mandarin | Tones only show how angry you are
exercise: In fast everyday talk, what often happens to the dipping tone? | It goes low and doesn't come back up | It turns into a high, level tone | It is shouted | It disappears completely from the word
exercise: Why does Mandarin sound «bossy» to some English ears? | It has many sharply falling words, but nobody is angry | Chinese people are always telling each other off | Every sentence ends with a shout | The fourth tone is only for orders
exercise: How do you say «I'm buying» and not «I'm selling»? | wǒ mǎi, with a dip | wǒ mài, with a fall | wǒ mā, high | wǒ ma, light
test: «ming says ask for táng at the shop, it means soup innit» What happens if you do? | You get sweets; soup is tāng | You get soup, he is right | You get a horse | You get told off for being rude
test: «yawn, wǒ yào shuǐjiǎo» you tell Ming's mum at 10 pm. What might she do? | Cheerfully offer you dumplings | Put you to bed straight away, it's late | Give you a glass of water | Phone your parents
test: You stop a stranger with «qǐng wěn» instead of qǐng wèn. Why might the class giggle? | You said «may I kiss» instead of «may I ask» | You said «please sell» | You said «please sleep» | You said «good night» in the morning
test: Counting 8, 9, 10 you say «bā, jiǔ, shǐ!». Why does Ming roll on the floor? | Dipping shǐ means poo, not ten | shǐ means horse | shǐ is a rude word for teachers | You counted backwards
test: What would happen if you called your mum mà instead of māma? | It would sound like «scold», not «mum» | Nothing, all four tones of ma mean mum | It would mean grandma | It would sound extra polite
test: In a cartoon an English kid shouts «nǐ hǎo?» with a rising voice to mean hello. What is off? | The rise at the end makes it sound like a question | Nothing, all greetings rise | nǐ hǎo is goodbye | Hello must always be whispered
test: «ugh, tones are just accent, nobody cares» Which is right? | Tones change the meaning, like vowels in English | Yes, only old people in the countryside use tones | Yes, tones are only for singing | Tones only matter in writing
test: At dim sum you ask for a leopard. What did you probably mean? | bāozi, a filled bun, said high | bàozi, said falling | mántou, a plain bun | tāng, a soup
test: A shopkeeper says wǒ mài. What is she doing? | Selling | Buying | Scolding you | Counting money
test: Ming's sister says «nǐ hǎo ma?» What is she asking? | Are you well? | Hello, Mum! | Are you a horse? | Where are you going?
test: Your friends make you say māma mà mǎ ma? fast and laugh. What is going on? | A friendly tone game, not mockery | They are being cruel and you should leave | They want you to scold a horse | It is a rude rhyme about mums
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an alteration in tone modifies the meaning of a Mandarin syllable. | The tonal contour constitutes an integral component of the word itself | Tonal variation merely conveys the emotional state of the speaker | Tones are employed exclusively in interrogative sentences | Tones are relevant solely to the written language
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the syllable shí when pronounced with a rising tone. | Ten | Excrement | To be | To lose
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the pair mǎi and mài. | The dipping form denotes purchasing, whereas the falling form denotes selling | Both forms denote purchasing | The dipping form denotes selling, whereas the falling form denotes purchasing | Both forms denote scolding
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the utterance «wǒ yào shuìjiào», both principal syllables bearing the falling tone? | It expresses a desire to sleep | It expresses a desire for boiled dumplings | It constitutes a request for sweets | It is an enquiry concerning the hour
